## Batch Job Scheduling

### Theory: Scheduling Concepts

**Why Scheduling?**

```
Scheduling optimizes:
├─ Mainframe CPU utilization
├─ I/O resource usage
├─ Job priority
├─ Peak load management
├─ Cost minimization
└─ SLA compliance

Scheduler tools:
├─ CA 7
├─ Control-M
├─ Workload Scheduler
└─ CA RSF1
```

**Common Scheduling Patterns:**

```
PATTERN 1: DAILY JOBS
├─ Run every night at 2:00 AM
├─ Example: End-of-day processing
├─ Must complete before business hours
└─ 6-hour window typical

PATTERN 2: WEEKLY JOBS
├─ Run Friday night
├─ Example: Payroll calculation
├─ Weekend processing acceptable
└─ Affects larger dataset

PATTERN 3: MONTHLY JOBS
├─ Run on specific date
├─ Example: Close accounting books
├─ Critical for compliance
└─ Resource-intensive

PATTERN 4: EVENT-TRIGGERED
├─ Run when condition met
├─ Example: Run if unprocessed records > 10000
├─ Avoids unnecessary processing
└─ Cost-saving

PATTERN 5: CONDITIONAL CHAINS
├─ Job2 waits for Job1 success
├─ Multiple dependencies
├─ Complex workflows
└─ Enterprise systems typical
```

**Example Scheduling Scenario:**

```
Monday-Friday Evening Schedule:
─────────────────────────────────

4:00 PM   Extract updated data
4:30 PM   Validate & clean
5:00 PM   Process transactions
5:30 PM   Calculate results
6:00 PM   Generate reports
6:30 PM   Distribute to users
7:00 PM   Backup critical data
7:30 PM   Log rotation & cleanup

Contingency:
├─ If Extract fails: Alert DBA, hold Validate
├─ If Validate fails: Generate error report, cancel Process
├─ If Process fails: Manual review required, restart available
├─ If results delayed > 30 min: Notify management

Friday Additional:
├─ 8:00 PM   Financial reconciliation
├─ 8:30 PM   Generate audit reports
├─ 9:00 PM   Archive weekly files
├─ 9:30 PM   Extended backup
```

