## Asynchronous Programming

### Q9: Explain Promises and async/await in AJAX context

**Answer:**

**Promises represent a value that may not be available yet but will be resolved eventually.**

**Promise states:**

```
PENDING → FULFILLED (success) → .then()
       ↘ REJECTED (error)    → .catch()
```

**Promise structure:**

```javascript
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    // Asynchronous operation
    const success = true;
    
    if (success) {
        resolve('Success!'); // FULFILLED
    } else {
        reject('Failed!'); // REJECTED
    }
});

promise
    .then(result => console.log(result))  // Handles fulfilled
    .catch(error => console.error(error)) // Handles rejected
    .finally(() => console.log('Done'));  // Always executes
```

**Fetch returns a Promise:**

```javascript
// Fetch API returns Promise<Response>
const responsePromise = fetch('/api/data');

// Chaining promises
fetch('/api/data')
    .then(response => {
        console.log('Got response');
        return response.json(); // Returns another Promise
    })
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Parsed data:', data);
        return data.id; // Returns resolved Promise
    })
    .then(id => {
        console.log('ID:', id);
        return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`); // Chain more requests
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(user => console.log('User:', user))
    .catch(error => console.error('Error somewhere:', error));
```

**Async/Await - cleaner syntax:**

```javascript
// async function returns a Promise
async function getUser(id) {
    try {
        // await pauses execution until Promise resolves
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
        
        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
        }
        
        const user = await response.json();
        return user;
        
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error:', error);
        throw error;
    }
}

// Call async function
getUser(1)
    .then(user => console.log('User:', user))
    .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));

// Or use await in another async function
async function displayUser() {
    try {
        const user = await getUser(1);
        console.log('User:', user);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    }
}
```

**Promise.all - parallel requests:**

```javascript
// Wait for multiple requests simultaneously
async function loadDashboard() {
    try {
        // All requests happen in parallel
        const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
            fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json()),
            fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),
            fetch('/api/comments').then(r => r.json())
        ]);
        
        console.log('Users:', users);
        console.log('Posts:', posts);
        console.log('Comments:', comments);
        
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error loading dashboard:', error);
    }
}

// If any Promise rejects, Promise.all rejects
// For fault-tolerance, use Promise.allSettled instead:
const results = await Promise.allSettled([
    fetch('/api/1').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/2').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/3').then(r => r.json())
]);

results.forEach((result, index) => {
    if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
        console.log(`Request ${index}:`, result.value);
    } else {
        console.error(`Request ${index} failed:`, result.reason);
    }
});
```

**Promise.race - fastest response:**

```javascript
// Returns first resolved/rejected promise
const fastestServer = await Promise.race([
    fetch('https://server1.com/api/data'),
    fetch('https://server2.com/api/data'),
    fetch('https://server3.com/api/data')
]);
```

