### Q10: How do you implement request cancellation with AbortController?

**Answer:**

AbortController allows you to cancel fetch requests.

**Why cancel requests:**

```
1. User navigates away from page
2. New request supersedes old one
3. User clicks "Cancel" button
4. Component unmounts (React, Vue)
5. Network timeout handling
```

**Basic implementation:**

```javascript
// Create abort controller
const controller = new AbortController();

// Pass signal to fetch
const response = await fetch('/api/data', {
    signal: controller.signal
});

// Cancel the request
controller.abort();
```

**Practical example:**

```javascript
class SearchAPI {
    constructor() {
        this.controller = null;
    }
    
    async search(query) {
        // Cancel previous search if in progress
        if (this.controller) {
            this.controller.abort();
        }
        
        // Create new controller for this search
        this.controller = new AbortController();
        
        try {
            const response = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, {
                signal: this.controller.signal
            });
            
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed');
            
            const results = await response.json();
            return results;
            
        } catch (error) {
            if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
                console.log('Search cancelled');
            } else {
                console.error('Search error:', error);
            }
            throw error;
        }
    }
    
    cancel() {
        if (this.controller) {
            this.controller.abort();
        }
    }
}

// Usage
const search = new SearchAPI();

// Trigger search
search.search('JavaScript').catch(() => {});

// User types more, cancel previous search
search.search('Java').catch(() => {});

// Only the last search completes
```

**Request timeout with AbortController:**

```javascript
function fetchWithTimeout(url, timeoutMs = 5000) {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    
    // Set timeout
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
        controller.abort();
    }, timeoutMs);
    
    return fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
        .then(response => {
            clearTimeout(timeoutId);
            return response;
        })
        .catch(error => {
            clearTimeout(timeoutId);
            if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
                throw new Error(`Request timeout after ${timeoutMs}ms`);
            }
            throw error;
        });
}

// Usage
try {
    const response = await fetchWithTimeout('/api/data', 3000);
    const data = await response.json();
} catch (error) {
    console.error(error.message); // "Request timeout after 3000ms"
}
```

**Multiple concurrent requests with grouped cancellation:**

```javascript
class RequestGroup {
    constructor() {
        this.controller = new AbortController();
    }
    
    async fetchMultiple(urls) {
        try {
            const promises = urls.map(url => 
                fetch(url, { signal: this.controller.signal })
                    .then(r => r.json())
            );
            
            return await Promise.all(promises);
            
        } catch (error) {
            if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
                console.log('All requests cancelled');
            } else {
                throw error;
            }
        }
    }
    
    cancel() {
        this.controller.abort();
    }
}

// Usage
const group = new RequestGroup();

group.fetchMultiple([
    '/api/users',
    '/api/posts',
    '/api/comments'
]).then(data => console.log(data));

// Cancel all requests at once
group.cancel();
```

